WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.310
The Pygmalion effect is the phenomenon
whereby higher expectations lead to

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.929
higher performance. It can be best
understood by a circle where our beliefs

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:14.250
about another person's abilities
influence our actions toward the other

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:20.069
person. This action has an impact on the other's beliefs about themselves. The

00:00:20.069 --> 00:00:24.359
beliefs about themselves cause the
others actions toward us, which again

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:34.170
reinforce our beliefs about that person.
And so on and on and on. Let's look at an

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.519
example and start with your beliefs.
Imagine you are the coach of a

00:00:38.519 --> 00:00:43.500
basketball team and you observe your
team on the first day: Chris and Joe are

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.269
new members of your team. Chris reminds
you of a famous player. Joe reminds you

00:00:48.269 --> 00:00:53.309
of an annoying boy from your high school
years. Unconsciously you decide what to

00:00:53.309 --> 00:01:01.319
expect of each one of them. Your beliefs
influence your actions: when Chris enters

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:05.610
the court you are happy to see him. When
he plays you push him to do better,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:11.130
practice harder, stay an extra hour. If he
makes a mistake you explained to him how

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.860
to improve. When Joe comes in you hardly
notice him. You're glad to see him score

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.689
but you don't give him much feedback and
don't invest extra time in his training.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:26.270
When Joe does a mistake
you are a little annoyed.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:32.479
Your actions impact their beliefs about
themselves: Chris feels you appreciate

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:38.689
him and he appreciates you in return. He
believes in his own success. Joe feels

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:43.130
you have little patience and
appreciation for him he does not believe

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:50.569
in his own success. Their beliefs about
themselves causes their actions toward

00:01:50.569 --> 00:01:55.250
you: Chris finds more and more joy and
playing and he never misses a training

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:00.679
session. During the games, he gives a
hundred percent all the time. Joe finds

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:03.979
less joy in playing than before and
doesn't give his full effort in the

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:12.140
games. He starts to miss the training
sessions sometimes. Which reinforces your

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:17.900
beliefs about them: you see how Chris
enjoys playing, how he trains hard and

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:23.510
shows a fast increase in his performance.
Joe seems not to be very motivated, his

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:28.220
skills don't increase as much and he
starts to show up less. You knew it right

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.590
away. Thank God your instincts were right!

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:37.940
The Pygmalion effect is also known as
the Rosenthal experiment named after a

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:43.370
research of Robert Rosenthal at Harvard.
In a first study, he challenged test

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.870
subjects to coach rats through a maze.
Half the group were told their rats were

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.190
extremely intelligent and specifically
trained, the other half were told that

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.970
their rats were dumb. In fact the rats
were all the same.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:00.859
During the experiment however the "smart"
rats performed well better than the "dumb"

00:03:00.859 --> 00:03:03.709
ones.
This showed how the expectations of the

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:08.230
coaches influenced even the performance
of rats.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:13.120
Rosenthal then did the Pygmalion in
school study together with Lenore

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.960
Jacobson: at the beginning of the school
year a group of elementary school

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.680
teachers were told that some of their
new pupils had extraordinary talent and

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.490
potential. This information which was
completely made up was given about

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:29.020
random average students in each class.
All students had done the IQ test in

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:32.830
advance. By the end of the year the
students that were described as more

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.610
talented had significantly increased
their performance in the IQ tests

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:43.240
compared to the rest of the class. Robert
Rosenthal concluded: "When we expect

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.870
certain behaviors of others we are
likely to act in ways that make the

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.040
expected behavior more likely to occur."
what do you think about this theory? And

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.760
if you believe it is there a way to
prevent ourselves from being shaped by

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:00.880
others in a negative way? If you like
